## 🔑 Key Findings

- **Risk_Review_Flag** is moderately imbalanced: 19.6% of transactions are flagged.
- Four transaction-level hypotheses were confirmed with high statistical significance: **international transactions**, **transfers/cash withdrawals**, **large amounts** (top decile), and **overnight transactions** (00:00–05:00) all show substantially higher flag rates.
- **Permutation importance overturned built-in feature importance.** Customer-level attributes (tenure, digital engagement score, account status, income band, prior flag rate) looked plausible early on but were confirmed — twice, across Week 3 and Week 4, using two different importance methods — to contribute nothing or actively hurt model performance. All five were formally removed in the final feature set.
- **`Transaction_Type` and transaction amount are the strongest predictors** of risk review, by a wide margin, in both the final Random Forest and a Logistic Regression cross-check.
- **Accuracy is a misleading headline metric for this problem.** The highest-"accuracy" model (Gradient Boosting, ~79%) caught only 5% of actually-flagged transactions. The final model trades some accuracy for a threshold tuned to catch ~79% of flagged transactions — the right trade-off for a manual-review use case, where a missed risk case is costlier than a false alarm.
- **Final model validated with no overfitting:** at the tuned decision threshold, recall was *identical* between the validation set (79.2%) and a completely held-out test set (79.2%), with all other metrics within ~2.6 percentage points.
- `Transaction_Status` was screened for label leakage via chi-square testing early in the project and deliberately excluded from every feature version.

